s_von_hippo","s":11,"p":[53,53],"r":[10,12,10,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. x. 12-14."},{"id":"9050df331122","v":"npnf.augustinus.epistulae","w":"augustinus.epistulae","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":47,"p":[21,21],"r":[6,19],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. vi. 19."},{"id":"21a58d38b441","v":"npnf.augustinus.epistulae","w":"augustinus.epistulae","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":93,"p":[35,35],"r":[22,9,22,12],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. xxii. 9-12."},{"id":"35f5f95589b8","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_civitate_dei","w":"augustinus.de_civitate_dei","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":407,"p":[0,0],"r":[24,2],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. xxiv. 2."},{"id":"4dccfa9c5166","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_peccatorum_meritis","w":"augustinus.de_peccatorum_meritis","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":129,"p":[0,0],"r":[23,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. xxiii. 6, 8."},{"id":"297a3fd96f8a","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_gratia_et_libero_arbitrio","w":"augustinus.de_gratia_et_libero_arbitrio","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":42,"p":[0,0],"r":[7,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"See Josh. vii. 4, 12."},{"id":"ca59279d7dcc","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_gratia_et_libero_arbitrio","w":"augustinus.de_gratia_et_libero_arbitrio","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":42,"p":[0,0],"r":[11,20],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. xi. 20."},{"id":"85cd10d023b8","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_sermone_domini","w":"augustinus.de_sermone_domini","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":18,"p":[0,0],"r":[9,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Augustin is somewhat perplexed about the meaning, but decides the injunction to be directed against the abuse of the oath, not to forbid it wholly. The oath was permitted by the law (Lev. xxii. 11), was to be held sacred (Num. xxx. 2), and to be made in God's name (Deut. vi. 13). It was customary under the Old Testament to swear (Gen. xxiv. 37, Josh. ix. 15; perhaps only a solemn affirmation), and in the name of the Lord (1 Sam. xx. 42; Irenaeus, Clement, Origen, Chrysostom, etc.). The Anabaptists, Mennonites, and Quakers understand the precept to forbid all oaths, even in the civil court. \"Christendom, if it were fully conformed to Christ's will, as it should be, would tolerate no oaths whatever\" (Meyer). \"The proper state of Christians is to require no oaths\" (Alford). If interpreted as a definite prohibition of all swearing, the passage comes into conflict with Christ's own example (Matt. xxvi. 63), and the apostle's conduct in the passages quoted by Augustin. The meaning has been restricted to rash and frivolous oaths on the street and in the market (Keim); in daily conversation (Carr, Camb. Bible for Schools ). In the ideal Christian community, where truth and honesty prevail, oaths will be superfluous: the simple asseverations, \"Yea, nay,\" will be sufficient. To this, Christ's precept ultimately looks. But He, no doubt, had in mind the widespread profanity of His day, and the current opinion that only oaths containing the name of God were binding (Lightfoot cites from the Rabbinical books to this effect). All unnecessary appeals to God, as well as careless and profane swearing, are forbidden, as coming either from bad passions within or a want of reverence. \"Prohibition would be repeal of the Mosaic law\" (Plumptre). \"All strengthening of the simple `Yea and nay 0' is occasioned by the presence of sin and Satan in the world. There is no more striking proof of the existence of evil than the prevalence of the foolish, low, useless habit of swearing. It could never have arisen if men did not believe each other to be liars,\" etc. (Schaff). \"Men use their protestations because they are distrustful one of another. An oath is physic, which supposes disease\" (M. Henry). When the oath is performed for the \"sake of ethical interests, as when the civil authority demands it,\" as seems to be necessary and safe for society in its present unsanctified condition, the precept does not interfere (Köstlin, art. \"Oath,\" Schaff-Herzog Encyl. , Meyer, Wuttke, Alford, Tholuck, etc.). An interesting imitation of the Rabbinical casuistry above referred to was practised by the crafty and subtle Louis XI. Scott says (Introd. to Quentin Durward ), \"He admitted to one or two peculiar forms of oath the force of a binding obligation which he denied to all others, strictly preserving the secret; which mode of swearing he really accounted obligatory, as one of the most valuable of State secrets.\""},{"id":"c09608e69c36","v":"npnf.augustinus.sermones_nt","w":"augustinus.sermones_nt","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":21,"p":[26,26],"r":[5,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. v. 3."},{"id":"1f857e683bbb","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":91,"p":[2,2],"r":[1,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"\"Jesus Nave\": Ιησοῦς (υἰὸς) Ναυῆ , Sept., Josh. i. 1."},{"id":"bae7becf514c","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":91,"p":[2,2],"r":[10,12,10,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. x. 12-14."},{"id":"5f2dded1ae39","v":"npnf.athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":29,"p":[1,1],"r":[1,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Gen. xv. 1; Gen. xxvi. 24; Exod. iv. 12, &c. Josh. i. 6."},{"id":"0a93df167e89","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p":[22,22],"r":[24,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Josh. xxiv. 23."},{"id":"e9f9585bc93e","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p